  "schema_version": 1,
  "description": "Which models may do governed work (ADR-0037). Governed mode runs only on frontier models: every ladder tier and the session's own model. One list, as data, read by the plugin (plugins/boopis/governance/frontier.py, which holds a copy made by scripts/sync-shared-data.sh) and by the host (apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/frontier.rs); both are tested against `examples`. A family is a model line with the lowest version that qualifies. To add a family, add one entry and its examples; nothing else changes. A family marked \"heavy\": true is frontier but token-heavy or costly: governed mode may run it, but only when the user asked for it. No ladder that setup writes and no default ladder, no automatic pick (Smart, the council's defaults) ever includes a heavy model; each heavy model has an opt-in toggle in Settings > Models, and a model the user names, mentions or adds is the user's own specification. To mark a family heavy later, add \"heavy\": true to its entry and a `heavy: true` example of it. Matching is by model, after normalising: lower case, the part after the last '/' (so a provider prefix and any vendor prefix such as anthropic/ or openrouter/anthropic/ fall away), a bracketed context suffix like [1m] dropped, dots and dashes in versions the same, a date suffix (20251101 or 2025-11-01) and a reasoning-effort suffix (minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, max) ignored. Anything else after the name means the model is not recognised, and a model that is not recognised is not frontier. Why these: Ramp's SWE-Bench v2 (500 private tasks from merged production PRs, pass@1, high effort), the owner's decision of 21 November 2026.",
